 the second line of defense against the vulnerability (independent of the feature-flag gate in reader.go), but there are no unit tests asserting these methods always fail, nor any test exercising `sql.TablesList`/`SQLCommand.Execute` end-to-end to confirm no code path can execute arbitrary SQL/filesystem access anymore. Given this is a direct mitigation for a critical CVE, this logic should be explicitly covered by tests rather than relying on incidental callers getting an error."}, {"file": "pkg/expr/reader.go", "line": 131, "severity": "low", "category": "doc_defect", "title": "Error message is misleading about why SQL expressions are unavailable", "body": "The error returned when `QueryTypeSQL` is requested — `"sqlExpressions is not implemented"` — is factually inaccurate: SQL expressions are implemented but intentionally disabled for security reasons (per the PR description, this is a hard-disable to close CVE-2024-9264), and the feature flag `FlagSqlExpressions` still exists and can be toggled with no effect. Returning a message implying the feature doesn't exist yet (rather than that it is disabled) can confuse users/operators who previously relied on it, and conflicts with any surviving documentation that still describes the flag as functional."}]}

findings.body:
The new helper function:

```go
func enableSqlExpressions(h *ExpressionQueryReader) bool {
	enabled := !h.features.IsEnabledGlobally(featuremgmt.FlagSqlExpressions)
	if enabled {
		return false
	}
	return false
}
```

computes a local variable named `enabled` as the *negation* of the feature flag state, then unconditionally returns `false` from both branches regardless of that variable's value. The function name and the variable name both suggest the SQL-expressions flag state is being consulted, but the flag value has zero effect on the result — the function always returns `false`. This is extremely easy for a future maintainer to misread as 'enabled mirrors the flag' and to 'simplify' into `return enabled` (which would silently re-enable the exact RCE/LFI vector this PR is meant to close) or to assume toggling `FlagSqlExpressions` has some effect when it does not. There is no comment explaining that this is an intentional hard kill-switch, which is a serious risk for a security-critical code path.
